# France - Introduction to Sports Medicine and Athletic Training (Cengage).
# Chapter list: 3rd edition, from the publisher's table of contents (ngl.cengage.com).
# Summaries cover each chapter's standard topics; they are written for this site, not copied from the book.

def ch(n, title, text, keys):
    return (n, title, [text], keys)

C = [
    ch(1, "Sports Medicine: The Multidisciplinary Approach to Athletic Health Care", "Sports medicine is a team approach to the health of physically active people. The team includes the team physician, certified athletic trainer, coach, physical therapist, orthopedist, strength and conditioning specialist, nutritionist, sports psychologist, and the athlete and family. The physician has final authority on medical decisions such as return to play.", ["Team approach", "Physician has final say on return to play", "Athletic trainer is the link", "Athlete is the center of the team"]),
    ch(2, "Athletic Training", "Athletic trainers (ATs) are licensed health care professionals who must graduate from an accredited program and pass the Board of Certification (BOC) exam. Their domains include injury prevention and wellness, examination and diagnosis, immediate and emergency care, therapeutic intervention, and health care administration. The NATA is their professional association.", ["BOC exam certifies ATs", "NATA = professional association", "Five practice domains", "ATs are health care professionals"]),
    ch(3, "The Central Training Room", "The athletic training facility needs areas for treatment, taping, rehabilitation, hydrotherapy, and storage, with good lighting, ventilation, and electrical safety (GFCI outlets near water). Policies cover sanitation, bloodborne pathogens, record keeping, and supplies. Records include injury reports, treatment logs, and pre-participation forms.", ["GFCI outlets near water", "Clean and sanitize daily", "Keep accurate records", "Bloodborne pathogen precautions"]),
    ch(4, "The Athletic Training Student Aide Program", "Student aides help the athletic trainer with tasks such as preparing water and supplies, taping under supervision, and maintaining the training room. They must work within their limits, keep confidentiality, and act professionally. Aides never diagnose or make return-to-play decisions.", ["Work under supervision", "Keep confidentiality", "Never diagnose", "Professional behavior"]),
    ch(5, "Emergency Preparedness: Injury Game Plan", "An emergency action plan (EAP) lists personnel roles, communication, equipment (AED, spine board), venue directions, and EMS access, and should be rehearsed. The primary survey checks airway, breathing, circulation (and severe bleeding); the secondary survey looks for other injuries. Never move a suspected spine injury; call EMS.", ["EAP for every venue, rehearsed", "Primary survey: ABCs", "Secondary survey after life threats", "Don't move suspected spine injuries"]),
    ch(6, "The Pre-Participation Physical Examination", "The pre-participation physical exam (PPE) screens athletes before sports by reviewing medical history and checking vital signs, vision, heart, and musculoskeletal health. Its goals are to find conditions that could put the athlete at risk (such as heart conditions linked to sudden cardiac death) and to clear athletes for play.", ["Medical history is most important", "Screens for sudden cardiac death risk", "Clears athletes for participation", "Done before the season"]),
    ch(7, "Prehabilitation and Preseason Conditioning", "Conditioning prevents injury by building strength, flexibility, endurance, and cardiovascular fitness. Principles include overload, progression, specificity (SAID), and rest. Warm-ups raise muscle temperature before activity, and cool-downs follow it. Stretching types include static, dynamic, ballistic, and PNF.", ["SAID principle: specificity", "Overload and progression", "Warm up before, cool down after", "PNF stretching"]),
    ch(8, "Nutrition and the Athlete", "Athletes need carbohydrates (main fuel), protein (repair), fats, vitamins, minerals, and water. Hydration is critical: drink before, during, and after activity and watch for dehydration. Pre-event meals should be eaten 3–4 hours before. Eating disorders, such as the female athlete triad (low energy availability, menstrual dysfunction, low bone density), are a concern.", ["Carbohydrates = main fuel", "Hydrate before, during, after", "Pre-event meal 3–4 hours before", "Female athlete triad"]),
    ch(9, "Dietary Supplements and Performance Enhancers", "Supplements are not tightly regulated by the FDA and may be contaminated. Performance-enhancing drugs such as anabolic steroids, stimulants, and blood doping are banned and harmful; steroid side effects include liver damage, acne, aggression, and heart problems. Creatine and caffeine are common legal supplements.", ["Supplements loosely regulated", "Anabolic steroids are dangerous", "Blood doping increases red cells", "Drug testing in sports"]),
    ch(10, "Sports Psychology", "Sports psychology addresses motivation, anxiety, focus, and the psychological response to injury. Techniques include goal setting, visualization (imagery), relaxation, and positive self-talk. Injured athletes may feel grief, anger, and depression, and need support during rehab.", ["Goal setting and imagery", "Manage anxiety", "Injury causes emotional response", "Positive self-talk"]),
    ch(11, "Assessment and Evaluation of Sports Injuries", "Injury evaluation follows HOPS: History, Observation, Palpation, Special tests (plus range of motion and strength). Injuries are acute or chronic. Sprains injure ligaments (grades I–III), and strains injure muscles or tendons. Signs of inflammation are redness, heat, swelling, pain, and loss of function.", ["HOPS evaluation", "Sprain = ligament; strain = muscle/tendon", "Grades I–III", "Signs of inflammation"]),
    ch(12, "Therapeutic Physical Modalities", "Modalities include cryotherapy (ice; first 24–72 hours to reduce swelling and pain), thermotherapy (heat; for chronic conditions, increases blood flow), ultrasound, electrical stimulation, and massage. PRICE (protection, rest, ice, compression, elevation) is the standard acute care. Heat is never used on new injuries.", ["PRICE for acute injuries", "Ice first, heat later", "Ultrasound and e-stim", "No heat on fresh injuries"]),
    ch(13, "Taping and Wrapping", "Taping supports and protects joints, limits harmful motion, and holds dressings. Common tapings include the ankle (closed basket weave), arch, wrist, thumb, and elbow hyperextension. Elastic wraps provide compression. Prepare skin, use underwrap, avoid wrinkles, and check circulation.", ["Ankle closed basket weave", "Check circulation after taping", "Underwrap protects skin", "Elastic wraps compress"]),
    ch(14, "Kinesiology", "Kinesiology is the study of human movement. Terms include flexion and extension, abduction and adduction, rotation, pronation and supination, and the anatomical position and planes (sagittal, frontal, transverse). Muscles act as agonists (prime movers) and antagonists.", ["Flexion/extension, abduction/adduction", "Sagittal, frontal, transverse planes", "Agonist vs. antagonist", "Pronation and supination"]),
    ch(15, "Bleeding and Shock", "Bleeding can be arterial (bright red, spurting), venous (dark red, steady), or capillary (oozing). Control bleeding with direct pressure, then a tourniquet for life-threatening limb bleeding. Shock is inadequate tissue perfusion; signs include pale, cool, clammy skin and a rapid, weak pulse. Treat by keeping the person warm and lying down and calling EMS.", ["Direct pressure first", "Arterial = bright red, spurting", "Shock: pale, cool, clammy, rapid pulse", "Keep warm, call EMS"]),
    ch(16, "The Bones and Soft Tissues", "Fractures can be open or closed and include types such as greenstick, transverse, spiral, comminuted, and stress fractures. Soft tissue injuries include contusions, abrasions, lacerations, and puncture wounds. Bones heal through inflammation, repair, and remodeling.", ["Open vs. closed fractures", "Stress fracture from overuse", "Contusion = bruise", "Splint fractures in place"]),
    ch(17, "The Foot, Ankle, and Lower Leg", "Common injuries include inversion ankle sprains (most often the anterior talofibular ligament), plantar fasciitis, Achilles tendinitis and rupture, shin splints (medial tibial stress syndrome), and compartment syndrome (an emergency).", ["Inversion sprain → ATF ligament", "Plantar fasciitis = heel pain", "Shin splints", "Compartment syndrome is an emergency"]),
    ch(18, "The Knee", "The knee has four main ligaments: ACL, PCL, MCL, and LCL. The ACL is often torn by non-contact cutting or landing (tested with Lachman's test). The menisci cushion the joint. Other problems include patellofemoral pain, patellar tendinitis (jumper's knee), and Osgood-Schlatter disease in adolescents.", ["ACL: Lachman's test", "MCL: valgus stress", "Menisci cushion the knee", "Osgood-Schlatter in teens"]),
    ch(19, "The Hip and Pelvis", "Injuries include hip pointers (contusion of the iliac crest), hamstring and groin (adductor) strains, hip dislocations, and stress fractures. The hamstrings are among the most commonly strained muscles.", ["Hip pointer = iliac crest bruise", "Hamstring strains common", "Groin = adductor strain", "Hip dislocation is an emergency"]),
    ch(20, "The Elbow, Wrist, and Hand", "Injuries include lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow), medial epicondylitis (golfer's elbow), UCL sprains in throwers, scaphoid fractures (tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox), carpal tunnel syndrome, mallet finger, and boxer's fracture (5th metacarpal).", ["Tennis elbow = lateral", "Scaphoid fracture: snuffbox pain", "Boxer's fracture = 5th metacarpal", "Mallet finger"]),
    ch(21, "The Shoulder", "The shoulder is very mobile but unstable. Injuries include anterior dislocations (most common), AC joint separations, rotator cuff strains and tears (SITS muscles: supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis), impingement, and clavicle fractures.", ["Anterior dislocation most common", "Rotator cuff = SITS", "AC separation", "Clavicle fractures"]),
    ch(22, "The Chest and Abdomen", "Chest injuries include rib fractures, pneumothorax, and commotio cordis (sudden cardiac arrest from a blow to the chest; use an AED). Abdominal injuries include spleen rupture (risk with mononucleosis; Kehr's sign is referred pain to the left shoulder) and kidney contusions.", ["Commotio cordis → AED", "Spleen rupture: Kehr's sign", "No contact sports with mono", "Pneumothorax = collapsed lung"]),
    ch(23, "The Head and Face", "A concussion is a traumatic brain injury; signs include headache, confusion, dizziness, and memory problems. Athletes with a suspected concussion are removed from play and must follow a graduated return-to-play protocol after medical clearance. Second impact syndrome can be fatal. Other injuries include facial fractures, nosebleeds, eye injuries, and dental injuries.", ["Remove from play if concussion suspected", "Graduated return-to-play", "Second impact syndrome", "Save knocked-out teeth in milk/saline"]),
    ch(24, "The Spine", "Spinal injuries can cause paralysis. Suspect a spine injury with neck pain, numbness, or tingling; stabilize the head and neck and activate EMS. In football, leave the helmet and shoulder pads on unless they must be removed. Other conditions include low back strains, herniated discs, and spondylolysis.", ["Stabilize head and neck", "Leave helmet and pads on", "Burners/stingers", "Herniated disc"]),
    ch(25, "Special Considerations in Athletes", "Special considerations include environmental conditions (heat illness, cold injuries, lightning: 30-30 rule), asthma, diabetes, epilepsy, skin infections (MRSA, herpes gladiatorum), and the needs of young, female, and disabled athletes. Exertional heat stroke is treated with cold-water immersion.", ["Heat stroke → cold-water immersion", "Lightning 30-30 rule", "MRSA and skin infections", "Asthma and diabetes plans"]),
]

